# Editable PowerPoint; each claim is based on the report above. Notes include a timed script.
slides=[
('Flight delay insurance','Programmable benefits. Explicit oracle trust.', ['CDS528 · Group project','Mock assets only · Local prototype','Report and code: '+REPO],45,'Introduce the team and the question: how can a traveller inspect payment rules and beneficiary rights? Explain that this is a classroom prototype with mock assets. It is not a deployed commercial insurance service.'),
('A precise problem','Travellers need predictable rules; LPs need visible exposure.', ['Traveller: who receives the benefit, and when?','LP: how much capital is reserved, and what is my share worth?','Smart contracts enforce custody; a reporter still supplies the outcome.'],50,'Define the users and distinguish the application from a conventional database. A database can implement the interface, but on-chain custody makes the implemented transfer conditions independently inspectable. Do not claim that an oracle makes external data automatically true.'),
('Six contracts, one workflow','On-chain value and permissions; off-chain interaction.', ['MockUSDC + InsurancePool → test asset, capital and shares','Insurance + PolicyNFT → terms, settlement and beneficiary','Oracle + PolicyMarket → reports and pre-departure sales'],60,'Walk through the architecture diagram. LPs supply capital. Travellers buy an NFT benefit. Insurance coordinates a pool and NFT. The Oracle reports a delay, while the marketplace changes the holder before departure. Frontend forms and date labels remain off-chain. Cite ERC-20, ERC-721 and OpenZeppelin.'),
('A policy has two different clocks','Time changes eligibility. A transaction finalises settlement.', ['Before departure → market sale allowed','Departure through end + 24 h → qualifying report allowed','Strictly after end + 24 h → refund may be triggered'],60,'Explain that raw Active does not change merely because time passed. The UI derives phases from latest chain time. A report or expiry transaction changes the final policy state and burns the NFT. At the final grace second reports are still accepted; refunds begin only after that instant.'),
('LP shares are not dollars','Net asset value and available liquidity answer different questions.', ['Net assets = balance − refundable reserve − pending risk fee','Available liquidity = balance − coverage reserve − refundable reserve','New shares ≈ deposit × total shares ÷ net assets'],70,'Describe the difference between ownership value and funds that may leave today. Unearned premiums do not count as LP profit. Locked coverage constrains withdrawals without mechanically repricing shares. Exact code uses integer arithmetic and one raw virtual unit in numerator and denominator.'),
('Why 3,941.7476 shares can be worth 2,030','A reconciled local example, not a return forecast.', ['2,000 deposit → 2,000 original shares','Two refunds + two claims → original assets fall to 1,030','Another 1,000 → 1,941.747571 new shares'],70,'Explain the arithmetic: two no-claim policies contribute 10; two claimed policies each lose 490 net, leaving 1,030. At about 0.515 per share, a 1,000 deposit buys 1,941.747571 shares. The original account totals 3,941.747571 shares worth 2,030. A later second-account deposit adds capital without creating a windfall for the first.'),
('Premium income does not guarantee profit','Fixed classroom pricing exposes a real economic limitation.', ['500 coverage → 10 premium → 5 refundable','No claim: pool gains 5 · Claim: pool loses 490 net','Simplified expected change = 5 − 495q'],55,'New LP deposits provide capital, not revenue. At this tariff and ignoring costs, break-even claim probability is about 1.01 percent. We have not estimated that probability. This is an economic limitation and a reason not to use real assets. Full reserves limit exposure but do not validate pricing.'),
('Security is layered','Permission checks, accounting invariants and atomic execution.', ['Oracle and threshold snapshotted for every policy','Roles + SafeERC20 + reentrancy guards','Live ownership + pre-departure check + buyer maxPrice'],60,'Discuss three threats: false or unauthorised reports, reentrant/token callbacks, and economic or stale-market abuse. State what the controls prevent and what they cannot prevent. The authorised oracle can still lie; privileged administration is a trust dependency.'),
('Evidence, with boundaries','20 contract tests · 22 frontend logic tests · successful build', ['Claims, refunds, permissions, reserves and market behaviour','Boundary seconds, snapshot terms, donation and callback attacks','Slither: 101 detectors; 11 retained findings'],60,'These are recorded runs from 11 November. They are not 42 browser tests or an independent audit. Slither retained two high, four medium, four low and one informational finding; six high/medium results were reviewed in context. Deterministic multi-policy tests are not exhaustive fuzzing. Cite the saved logs and security review.'),
('Demonstrate the complete lifecycle','Use two policies: one claim path and one refund path.', ['LP approval + deposit → traveller approval + purchase','Query policy → authorised report → current holder receives claim','Separate policy → grace period ends → expiry refund'],75,'Use the local launcher and pre-funded test wallets. In operations query a policy and verify its beneficiary before signing. Advance local debug time only as required, noting all policies are affected. Submit a qualifying report. For a second policy, cross the full grace window and trigger refund. If the live wallet or chain fails, show previously captured evidence honestly instead of pretending a transaction succeeded.'),
('Deployment status is part of the result','Local implementation complete; testnet evidence still pending.', ['Verified locally: deployment, tests and build','Not yet evidenced: Sepolia + explorer source verification','Before submission: public repo, updated code and successful CI'],60,'Be candid about the R5 gap. Do not show local addresses as Sepolia addresses. Explain the immutable-contract rationale: pause new purchases, preserve existing exits, and deploy a replacement if needed. The teacher requires a public repository, so current access settings must be checked before submission.'),
('What we learned','Custody rules can be automated; external truth remains a dependency.', ['Value: inspectable terms, ownership and reserve accounting','Limits: manual oracle, teaching tariff and unfinished testnet proof','Next: live-data trust model, calibrated pricing and browser acceptance'],55,'Conclude with the strongest supported contribution and the most important unresolved risks. Mention upstream adaptation and AI assistance. Ask each group member to speak during the presentation. Invite questions on oracle truth, LP losses, direct NFT transfer versus market rules, and why expiry still requires a transaction.'),
('References & review checklist','Appendix · not included in the 12-minute speaking budget', ['ERC-20 (2015) · ERC-721 (2018) · OpenZeppelin Contracts','Hardhat 3 documentation · Crytic Slither','Upstream: GtiHuberZZ/insurance-dapp · AI: ChatGPT/Codex'],0,'Official URLs and APA-style references appear in the report. Before submission add real names and student IDs, verify upstream licensing, make the repository public, and review the AI disclosure. Do not claim Sepolia success until deployment and verification links exist.')]

